\begin{abstract}
This report details the design and development of Neuro-Drive, an advanced Internet of Things (IoT)-based active safety system designed to detect driver drowsiness and automate emergency rescue protocols. The system utilizes a dual-node architecture consisting of an ESP32-CAM and a primary ESP32 microcontroller. The vision node wirelessly streams real-time video to a local Python environment, which uses OpenCV to calculate Eye Aspect Ratios (EAR) for lag-free fatigue detection. The core processing node handles continuous biometric tracking (Heart Rate and SpO2) using a MAX30102 sensor. Upon detecting driver fatigue, the system initiates a multi-stage physical alert using a 0.96-inch OLED, an active buzzer, and a DC vibration motor powered by an isolated 5V Li-ion battery. Simultaneously, utilizing FreeRTOS dual-core processing, the system performs background reverse-geocoding via Wi-Fi and retrieves exact satellite coordinates via a NEO-6M GPS module. A SIM900A GSM module is then triggered to dispatch an automated SOS SMS containing the driver's vitals, a localized Zone name, and a Google Maps link to predefined authorities. This project demonstrates a highly cost-effective, edge-computed Advanced Driver Assistance System (ADAS) that enhances road safety and rescue efficiency.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
IoT, Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS), ESP32, ESP32-CAM, FreeRTOS, MAX30102, OpenCV, NEO-6M GPS, SIM900A GSM, Haptic Feedback, Biometric Monitoring.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Project Overview}
Driver drowsiness is a critical factor in global highway fatalities. While commercial fatigue detection systems exist, they are often prohibitively expensive and limited to high-end vehicles. This project introduces Neuro-Drive, an affordable, edge-computed assistive node developed to support driver safety through vision-based monitoring, physical haptic feedback, and automated emergency alerts.

The system relies on an ESP32-CAM to capture the driver's face, passing the stream to a Python script that analyzes facial landmarks. If prolonged eye closure is detected, an HTTP GET request triggers the primary ESP32. The main ESP32 immediately activates a synchronized alarm: an OLED visual warning, an active buzzer, and an isolated DC vibration motor for intense haptic feedback. 

In emergencies, the system relies on a SIM900A GSM module to send an automated SOS message. To optimize the data, the ESP32 operates on a FreeRTOS dual-core architecture. Core 1 handles the real-time biometric tracking (via MAX30102) and hardware interrupts, while Core 0 runs background HTTP requests to a reverse-geocoding API to fetch the local ``Zone'' name. By combining computer vision, real-time biometric sensing, and IoT communication, Neuro-Drive offers a robust safety net for logistics and heavy machinery operators.

