\section{Features}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Vision-Based Fatigue Detection:} The system utilizes OpenCV and Dlib facial landmark mapping to calculate the Eye Aspect Ratio (EAR) in real-time, triggering alarms instantly upon detecting microsleeps.
    \item \textbf{Authentic Biometric Node:} Equipped with the MAX30102 sensor, the node continuously monitors physiological metrics, allowing rescue teams to assess the driver's health state remotely.
    \item \textbf{Automated SOS Emergency System:} When fatigue is detected, the system automatically sends a compiled SOS message via the SIM900A GSM module to predefined contacts, protected by a 3-minute anti-spam cooldown.
    \item \textbf{Real-Time Location \& Reverse Geocoding:} The system combines hardware NEO-6M satellite tracking with FreeRTOS background API calls to provide both exact coordinates and readable geographical ``Zone'' names.
    \item \textbf{Multi-Stage Physical Alerts:} To maximize the chances of waking the driver, the system simultaneously deploys visual (OLED), auditory (Buzzer), and haptic (Vibration Motor) feedback.
\end{enumerate}

\section{System Functionality and Control Logic}
The system is organized into two core functional domains, distributed across the dual-core architecture of the ESP32 to ensure lag-free operation.

\subsection{Assistive Alert and Biometric Control (Core 1)}
The ESP32's primary core serves as the central unit for environmental sensing and physical alerts.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Biometrics \& UI:} The loop continuously reads the infrared value from the MAX30102. If a finger is detected, it calculates the delta between beats to render BPM and SpO2 onto the dynamic OLED interface.
    \item \textbf{Haptic \& Audio Feedback:} Upon receiving the HTTP \texttt{/alarm} trigger, Core 1 executes a synchronous loop. It writes \texttt{HIGH} to GPIO 13 (Buzzer) and GPIO 25 (Vibration Transistor Base) for 150\,ms intervals, creating an intense, pulsed waking sequence.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Background Networking and SOS Dispatch (Core 0)}
To avoid interrupting the critical biometric and alert loops, networking tasks are pinned strictly to Core 0 using the \texttt{xTaskCreatePinnedToCore} FreeRTOS function.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Reverse Geocoding:} Core 0 wakes every 15 seconds to execute an HTTP GET request to the BigDataCloud API, parsing JSON payloads to extract the local city/locality string.
    \item \textbf{Emergency Dispatch:} When triggered by Core 1, the SIM900A transmits AT commands to dispatch the cellular payload containing the Biometrics, the Zone string, and the Google Maps link formulated from the NEO-6M's NMEA sentences.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
Neuro-Drive successfully integrates wireless computer vision, biometric sensing, and IoT communication to assist and protect drivers in real-time. By combining the ESP32 microcontroller with a vibration feedback circuit, GPS-based location tracking, and an SOS alert system, the device ensures a highly responsive safety layer. Overall, the project demonstrates how affordable and compact electronic components can be integrated using advanced software techniques like FreeRTOS to create a life-saving vehicular node. 

\subsection{Future Possibilities}
In the future, Neuro-Drive can be further enhanced with advanced technologies such as:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Vehicle CAN Bus Integration:} Direct interfacing with the vehicle's onboard computer to safely and autonomously decelerate the engine if the driver is unresponsive.
    \item \textbf{Mobile Application \& IoT Dashboard:} Creating a companion web dashboard for logistics fleet managers to remotely track the real-time location, fatigue events, and vitals of all active drivers in their network.
    \item \textbf{AI-Based Emotion Recognition:} Expanding the Python vision model to detect yawning, distraction, or distress markers alongside standard drowsiness.
\end{itemize}

